 "clinical_assessment": "The primary hazard arises from the concurrent use of terazosin (alpha-1 adrenergic antagonist) with three CNS depressants - nightly trazodone, lorazepam, and sertraline - plus a daily glass of wine. This polypharmacy creates additive pharmacodynamic depression of the central nervous system and vasculature.",
 "drug_rule": "Terazosin blocks alpha-1 receptors, reducing peripheral vascular resistance and causing orthostatic hypotension. Trazodone, lorazepam, and sertraline each depress the CNS via distinct mechanisms (serotonin reuptake inhibition, GABA potentiation, and weak histamine antagonism). Alcohol further amplifies sedation and impairs motor coordination. The combination produces synergistic hypotension and excessive sedation, increasing risks of falls, syncope, and respiratory depression.",
 "patient_fit": "The patient's advanced age (74 years) and mild renal impairment reduce compensatory mechanisms against hypotension. The prescribed bedtime dose of terazosin, combined with the patient's existing nightly regimen of three CNS depressants and alcohol, overrides any general safety assumptions about a standard starting dose. The additive effects on blood pressure and sedation are clinically unacceptable, necessitating the \"Unsafe\" verdict.",
 "risk_map": {
